F5'S HASKELL TWIN -- and the sweep that should have caught it ran and reported
clean.

Network.Wai.{responseLBS, responseFile, responseBuilder, responseStream}
shipped under net_recv. They BUILD the payload a server is about to send, so
each minted untrusted_input at a call that observes nothing -- and, the more
serious half, no Wai row was a SINK, so a Warp application had no egress
surface for its own response path and a secret written into a response body
could not be reported as leaving the process. The failure direction is REPORTS
SAFE, not reports noise.

THE INSTRUMENT WAS BLIND, WHICH IS THE STRUCTURAL HALF. The F6 direction sweep
matches primitive names against a direction vocabulary. That vocabulary carried
the VERB `respond` and not the NOUN `response`, so responseLBS matched nothing
in any of the fifteen catalogues. A name-based gate with an incomplete
vocabulary does not fail; it exits 0. The vocabulary gains response/resp/render
and is hoisted to module scope so a test can interrogate it rather than trust
it. The widening is deliberately TIGHT: a generous one was measured first and
returned 43 extra hits, every single one a false alarm -- an HTTP client's
request() sends, wait_with_output reads, ServerBootstrap merely contains
"Serve". The tight one finds exactly these four rows and nothing else.

WARP'S LAUNCH ROWS ARE DELIBERATELY KEPT RECEIVES. Warp.{run, runSettings,
runTLS, runEnv} pose exactly the question Phoenix.Router poses, which the F5
change kept as a control: arrival has no call site to catalogue, so moving them
would relocate the receive to NOTHING. The item forbids adjudicating the two
apart, and this is its "or neither" branch. Enumerating the family turned up 37
rows across NINE languages, answered three separate times by the same argument
and never once actually ruled -- filed as WI-johuk for one adjudication, and
pinned meanwhile by a cross-language test so it cannot be split silently.

MEASURED A/B on 3 Wai/Warp repos (postgrest, servant, yesod): per-arm cache,
arms sequential, canary on the LOADED Network.Wai boundary (NEW net_send / OLD
net_recv), against a NETWORK claim set -- the standing claims file is
host_fs-only and is structurally incapable of seeing this change at all.

  net_recv chains   27 -> 9    (-18)
  net_send chains   18 -> 36   (+18)
  ten other boundaries         IDENTICAL

Accounted CHAIN BY CHAIN rather than inferred from the symmetry: the 18 Wai
chains changed direction, the 9 Warp launch chains did not move, and the 18
pre-existing HTTP.Client sinks did not move.

  yesod  UNTRUSTED-NO-NETWORK   inconclusive(0) -> violated(4)

Two of those four sink in production source (Devel.hs:reverseProxy,
Dispatch.hs:toWaiAppYre); two sink inside a test file, so the honest count is
two, not four. servant holds at violated(3) on both claims as the in-run
control, and yesod's HOSTSECRET claim holds at inconclusive as a second.

DIRECTION: this change REPORTS MORE. It adds sinks, and the removals it
performs delete false sources. Both halves move away from the all-clear.

FOUND WHILE ENUMERATING THE LAUNCH FAMILY AND NOT ADDRESSED HERE: go's
syscall.{Socket,Bind,Listen}, unix.{Socket,Bind,Listen}, net.Listen and
fiber.App.Listen are still net_recv -- the INV-nular F2 family that was
supposed to be extinguished. The gate enforcing F2 is blind to Go twice over:
it parametrises over a hardcoded nine-language list that omits Go, AND its
predicate is case-sensitive while Go capitalises every exported identifier, so
adding Go to that list would still report clean. Verified rather than assumed.
Filed as INV-kanuk, violated, P1, sequenced behind WI-johuk.

